es buildings through joint ventures. FRP Holdings, Inc. was founded in 1988 and is based in Jacksonville, Florida.","ceo_bio":"Mr. John D. Baker III | CEO & Director | Age 39 | Compensation: $799,800 (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 25 people. Revenue per employee of $1.7M ranks in the below the sector median among Real Estate peers, with revenue falling 7%/yr across its filed history. Stock-based compensation of $84K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 0\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["FRP Holdings, Inc. is a small-cap real estate business with a $355M market cap on $43M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +5% over the past three years and +2% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 1% against a 6% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 39%.","The company is burning through $21M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 10.5\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 2.4\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.1%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 40% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.9) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Carries a 193% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.9308359133126936},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Interest-rate sensitivity and tenant-credit risk \u2014 cap rates and refinancing costs move faster than the 10-year leases can absorb.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Remote work structural vacancy \u2014 the post-pandemic shift to hybrid work has permanently impaired demand for urban office space across most markets.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Climate and insurance cost escalation \u2014 rising premiums and coverage withdrawals in flood/fire zones are repricing assets that the market once treated as perpetuities.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Insiders are net buyers (100% buy ratio) \u2014 insiders sell for many reasons but buy for only one","Large open-market insider purchases (net $10.4M) \u2014 management putting its own capital behind the business","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Understanding Momentum Shifts in (FRPH)","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/90/Understanding_Momentum_Shifts_in_FRPH_111126092401_1794407041.html","date":"2026-11-11","timestamp":1794407041.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"What FRP (FRPH) Said on Its Q3 Earnings Call","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/07/what-frp-frph-said-on-its-q3-earnings-call.html","date":"2026-11-08","timestamp":1794100524.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.612,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"FRP Holdings (FRPH) Q3 2026 Earnings Call Transcript","source